1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,960
Well, good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:05,000
How are we doing today?

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,920
I think, man, what a wake-up call this Sunday.

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,800
I mean, confession, great worship set.

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,340
I'm just happy to be here with you guys.

6
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:14,980
If you don't know me, if we haven't been introduced,

7
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:17,600
my name is Corey Smith, and I have the privilege

8
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,480
of being on the teaching team here at Canvas Community.

9
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,960
So it's my privilege to be able to bring you

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:23,960
the message this morning.

11
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:28,240
So we're kind of in our home stretch of our summer series,

12
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:30,940
a summer's journey, and that kind of follows

13
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:32,800
the life and teachings of the Apostle Paul.

14
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:37,200
Has anyone else this summer, like, dramatically flown by?

15
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,540
Like, in my mind, we're in April,

16
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:40,560
and we're, like, in August already.

17
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:43,300
But here we are, right?

18
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:46,520
So in our later section, if you've kind of guessed,

19
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:48,300
we've kind of changed gears just a little bit,

20
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:50,440
and we're kind of zeroing in on some of the theological

21
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:52,920
implications of the Apostle Paul's teachings.

22
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:56,640
A couple weeks ago, Ryan preached a great message

23
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:01,040
on unity and what are the negotiables and non-negotiables

24
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:04,620
when it comes to unity within the body of Christ

25
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:07,320
and how we as the church have tended to divide

26
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,060
over negotiable things and not necessarily

27
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:10,840
the non-negotiables.

28
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:13,460
Last week, Ryan again brought us a message

29
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:16,080
on church discipline, better called church restoration,

30
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:18,180
where the Apostle Paul taught us

31
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,480
and the church of Corinth, right,

32
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,360
how to call out sin for what it is,

33
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:24,680
how to do our best to seek it out

34
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:26,800
so that a little yeast doesn't ruin the whole batch.

35
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:30,760
Ultimately, right, it's kind of the crux of that sermon.

36
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:32,580
Ultimately, for the good of the person,

37
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:34,600
the good of creation,

38
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,120
and the good of being reconciled back to Jesus Christ.

39
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:40,900
But today, I want to kind of ask the question,

40
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:44,620
sure, unity, restoration,

41
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,840
they sound like good things.

42
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:50,540
But ultimately, why do these things matter?

43
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:54,060
What drives us to unity?

44
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:57,280
What drives us to holiness?

45
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:00,240
And I'll kind of give you my response to that question

46
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:01,660
because I know you're itching to know.

47
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:05,960
But my response to that question

48
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:07,080
of why does unity matter,

49
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:08,560
why does holiness matter,

50
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:12,440
is really encapsulated in one central event.

51
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:16,140
That central event is the resurrection of Jesus Christ.

52
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:20,500
The late Cardinal Basil Hume said this,

53
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:23,080
every parting gives a foretaste of death,

54
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:25,200
every coming together,

55
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:27,160
again, the foretaste of the resurrection.

56
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:30,400
So today, we're going to talk about faithful lives

57
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,100
amid the persecution and light and hope of the resurrection.

58
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:35,560
But first, I want to set the stage, right?

59
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:36,200
That's my MO.

60
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,320
Not with historical context.

61
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:39,480
You're welcome.

62
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:40,100
That'll come later.

63
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,880
But I want to set the stage in this way.

64
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:46,280
And it's something we do every day.

65
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:48,520
It's something we all do.

66
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:50,860
Most of us probably don't even know we do it.

67
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:52,440
And in fact, we do this in basically

68
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:53,780
every conversation we have,

69
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:54,880
every interaction.

70
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,440
It can be the foundation of your highest joys

71
00:02:58,440 --> 00:02:59,720
and your deepest sorrows.

72
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:02,240
It's the reasons you make the decisions you make

73
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:04,760
and why you respond the way you do in situations.

74
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:07,400
It's an intensely human thing to do.

75
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:11,280
Is anybody, like, convicted on what, like,

76
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:14,600
do we each have our own little nuance to that?

77
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:15,680
But what I'm talking about,

78
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:17,280
every day and probably every situation,

79
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:18,120
every decision,

80
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:21,020
you and I interpret the reality of our,

81
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:24,440
we interpret reality through the lens of our worldview.

82
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:27,660
This is how you and I make sense of what's going on.

83
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:30,500
No one, absolutely no one, I would argue,

84
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,800
lives life based on the raw factual realities of their lives.

85
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:38,980
But instead, they live life based on the interpretation of those facts

86
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:42,040
and that reality through the lens of their worldview.

87
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:45,220
Two people can come to the exact same situation,

88
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:46,960
the exact same factual reality,

89
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:48,540
and come out with two different conclusions.

90
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:54,240
Our interpretation of reality is really our lens in which we navigate the world.

91
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:55,820
See, we all have lenses.

92
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:58,140
We all interpret reality.

93
00:03:58,140 --> 00:03:59,680
That's the first acknowledgement.

94
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:00,320
Am I on?

95
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:01,700
I'm touching on base?

96
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:02,420
Is everybody with me?

97
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:02,660
Okay.

98
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:07,600
I think it's how we make sense of our lives and the situations they're in.

99
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:09,540
In Christianity, we've put a label on it, right?

100
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:10,360
We've called it faith.

101
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:12,840
But I think there's a misconception floating around

102
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:15,680
that Christians are the only ones who live by faith.

103
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:17,180
And I would argue that everyone does,

104
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:19,400
from the most agnostic or irreligious person

105
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:22,560
to devout Christians, spiritualists, name your flavor.

106
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:24,800
We all live by faith.

107
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:26,240
And what I mean by that is

108
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:32,100
we live our lives driven by our interpretation of reality

109
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:33,140
based on our worldview.

110
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:34,340
That's our faith.

111
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:34,960
Does that make sense?

112
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:38,540
So faith is not something you muster up.

113
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,960
Faith is not something that's an intellectual agreement.

114
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:42,620
Faith for everyone is viewing

115
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,480
and interpreting the world through a certain lens

116
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:48,220
and then acting upon those situations

117
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:49,860
based on that understanding and worldview.

118
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:52,540
See, faith is an allegiance to something

119
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:55,540
that drives you to act upon that interpretation of reality.

120
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:59,260
So I think Christians aren't different because we live by faith.

121
00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:02,460
Christians are different because of the object of our faith.

122
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:05,400
See, the word faith isn't, it kind of is,

123
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:08,020
but there's root words to the word faith.

124
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:10,200
And it's not something that Christianity or religion made up.

125
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:12,440
It's kind of this mindset where it's a practice

126
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:14,120
and a word that we've used as mankind

127
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:17,200
to help us describe and verbalize

128
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:20,940
the interpretations of situations in light of our worldview.

129
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:27,740
And our worldview as Christians should be categorically centered around one individual.

130
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:29,940
Give me the Sunday school answer.

131
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:31,220
Jesus.

132
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:34,060
And one primary act, the resurrection.

133
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:38,740
So all Christian creeds, doctrines, and religious systems across Christendom

134
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:42,760
rest on this central phenomenon by God stepping into his creation

135
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:44,020
and redeeming what was lost.

136
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:45,900
See, Jesus died on the cross.

137
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:50,080
And that sacrificial gift was an immense thing, right?

138
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:53,460
But Jesus died in his humanity as any of us would.

139
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:59,740
But, I hope that makes sense for you guys.

140
00:05:59,740 --> 00:06:03,360
But through divine mystery of God interacting with his creation,

141
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:06,820
Jesus was brought back bodily in a glorified state three days later.

142
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:09,160
This is the resurrection of Jesus Christ.

143
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:11,960
This is why the teachings of Jesus carry weight.

144
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:16,040
This is why the cross was victorious in its conquest of sin

145
00:06:16,040 --> 00:06:18,880
because Jesus arose triumphant over his enemies.

146
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:23,080
And he's back to let the world know how victorious and triumphant he was.

147
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:27,920
And there really are, through today, there's going to be two resurrections I'm talking about.

148
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:33,600
One, Jesus' resurrection, which was that statement of triumphant victory over sin.

149
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:37,200
And two, our eventual resurrection, the one that mirrors our Lord's.

150
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:41,520
And we'll touch on this later, but it's Jesus being the first fruits of the harvest.

151
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:46,720
And the first fruits would have been what you would have dedicated or given to the temple as tithe.

152
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:48,260
And that belonged to God.

153
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:51,440
And the rest of the harvest was yours.

154
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:53,740
So, Jesus is the first fruits of our resurrection.

155
00:06:53,740 --> 00:06:57,060
Jesus' resurrection is the down payment and assurance

156
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:01,660
that the renewal of all creation will follow.

157
00:07:01,660 --> 00:07:04,260
This is the theme of my message.

158
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:07,000
Faithful lives amid persecution in the light.

159
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:08,260
And hope of the resurrection.

160
00:07:08,260 --> 00:07:11,320
I hope to work through two questions today.

161
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:15,660
That not only have theological application, but also have practical application.

162
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:19,060
One, what is the ultimate Christian hope?

163
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:24,140
And two, what hope is there for change, rescue, transformation,

164
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:26,300
new possibilities within the world of the present?

165
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:28,180
So, for my note takers, write those down.

166
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:31,680
And I want you to think about these as we go through the lesson today.

167
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:33,940
How would you answer these two questions?

168
00:07:33,940 --> 00:07:38,040
I know how I would answer them, but I also wrote a three-hour long sermon around them.

169
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,620
I was wondering how gun-shy Ryan had you guys.

170
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:47,760
But seriously, how would we answer these questions?

171
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:50,800
And not just now in our comfortable seats, given Sunday school answers,

172
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:56,780
but in the midst of sorrow, grief, tragedy, death, persecution,

173
00:07:57,460 --> 00:07:59,640
how would we answer these questions?

174
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:02,100
And would they be something that we could stand by?

175
00:08:02,100 --> 00:08:07,420
So, would our answers to these questions give us faith, right?

176
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:12,040
The interpretation of our worldview to stand in the face of tragedy,

177
00:08:12,040 --> 00:08:14,360
persecution, of sorrow, of death.

178
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:15,700
Let's pray together.

179
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,240
God, our Father, give us ears to hear and hearts to obey

180
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:23,400
what you would have us take away from this message today, Lord.

181
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:26,760
Let no one hear my words, but hear only your truth

182
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,400
within my feeble attempt to deliver it.

183
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:32,060
Give us renewed hope in you as the author and perfecter of our faith.

184
00:08:32,060 --> 00:08:34,340
Give us eyes to see creation through the lens of Jesus

185
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:36,740
in light of the hope of the resurrection.

186
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:39,980
Give us hearts to see people not as enemies,

187
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:42,660
but those in bondage who don't realize they're in bondage.

188
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:45,880
Give us eyes to see each other through the lens of the resurrection,

189
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:50,400
this event that changes our interpretation of the world and of each other.

190
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:54,920
Give us hands that seek to do the will of you, King Jesus.

191
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:58,480
We pray all of this for your glory and our neighbor's good.

192
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:00,940
In Jesus' precious name we pray, amen.

193
00:09:00,940 --> 00:09:04,540
Today, our Scripture reading is going to be primarily,

194
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:05,940
I've got a lot of Scripture reading today,

195
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:10,620
primarily out of 1 Corinthians and 1 Thessalonians,

196
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:12,840
but what I really want to do is give a 101,

197
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:15,200
so that we're all on this, a 101 on resurrection.

198
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:18,080
That way we're all on the same page, we're all hearing the same thing,

199
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:20,120
and we can talk about that later, right?

200
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:23,340
That's why we'll be turning first to the gospel of Luke,

201
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:25,600
more specifically Luke 24.

202
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:32,280
If you'll recall, Luke is the author of the gospel bearing his name.

203
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:34,640
He's also the author of the book of Acts,

204
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:37,440
which we've been going through, remember, one long narrative.

205
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:39,800
In resurrection, you'll see,

206
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:42,660
if you look at it as a one long narrative mindset,

207
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:47,680
from the gospel, from Jesus' birth to the resurrection,

208
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:50,880
to the apostles taking the gospel out to the four corners of the earth,

209
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:52,480
you'll see this as one long narrative,

210
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:55,260
and the linchpin of that is the resurrection.

211
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:59,560
And I'm afraid, here's my fear,

212
00:09:59,560 --> 00:10:01,120
and I could be projecting my fear,

213
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:02,240
and I'll fully realize that.

214
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:05,480
My fear is that we've boiled down resurrection to just Easter Sunday.

215
00:10:05,480 --> 00:10:08,640
But this central phenomenon sets the stage

216
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:10,240
for the church going out into all the world.

217
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:12,180
My goal today in this lesson

218
00:10:12,180 --> 00:10:14,620
is to renew our eyes on the single event

219
00:10:14,620 --> 00:10:16,540
that we should use to set our worldview

220
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:20,000
and to use to interpret the world around us

221
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,120
and the events we encounter every single day.

222
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:23,980
Let's read Luke 24 together.

223
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:27,440
On the first morning of the week,

224
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:28,520
very early in the morning,

225
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:31,260
they came to the tomb bringing the spices they had prepared.

226
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:33,460
They found the stone rolled away from the tomb,

227
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:35,980
and they went in but did not find the body of the Lord Jesus.

228
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:38,120
While they were perplexed about this,

229
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:40,840
suddenly two men stood by them in dazzling clothes.

230
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:44,000
So the women were terrified and bowed down to the ground.

231
00:10:45,140 --> 00:10:49,080
Why are you looking for the living among the dead?

232
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:50,500
Asked the men.

233
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,300
He is not here, but he is risen.

234
00:10:52,300 --> 00:10:56,560
Remember how he spoke to you when he was still in Galilee,

235
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:56,900
saying,

236
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:59,200
This is necessary that the Son of Man be betrayed

237
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:00,640
into the hands of sinful men,

238
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:02,820
be crucified, and rise on the third day.

239
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:04,500
And they remembered his words.

240
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:05,880
Returning from the tomb,

241
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:07,720
they reported all these things to the eleven,

242
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:09,780
and to the rest, Mary Magdalene, Joanna,

243
00:11:09,780 --> 00:11:11,480
Mary, the mother of James,

244
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:13,160
and the other women with them

245
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:14,900
were telling the apostles these things.

246
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,580
These words seemed like nonsense to them.

247
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:20,000
They did not believe the women.

248
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,640
Peter, however, got up and ran to the tomb.

249
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:24,020
When he stooped to look in,

250
00:11:24,020 --> 00:11:26,680
he saw only the linen clothes.

251
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:29,140
So he went away amazed at what had happened.

252
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:31,540
Now, that same day,

253
00:11:31,540 --> 00:11:34,060
two of them were on their way to a village called Emmaus,

254
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:36,260
which is about seven miles from Jerusalem.

255
00:11:36,260 --> 00:11:39,420
Together, they were discussing everything that had taken place.

256
00:11:39,420 --> 00:11:41,560
And everything they were discussing and arguing,

257
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:44,120
Jesus came near and began to walk along with them.

258
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:47,660
But they were prevented from recognizing him.

259
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:49,040
Then he asked them,

260
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:51,900
What is this dispute that you're having with each other as you're walking?

261
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:54,300
And they stopped walking and looked discouraged.

262
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:56,560
The one named Cleopas answered him and said,

263
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:58,720
Are you the only visitor in Jerusalem

264
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:01,260
who doesn't know about the things that had happened there in these days?

265
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:02,280
What things?

266
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:02,800
He asked them.

267
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:21,900
Besides all of this, it's the third day since these things happened.

268
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:24,100
Moreover, some women from our group astounded us.

269
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:25,620
They arrived early at the tomb,

270
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:27,340
and they didn't find his body.

271
00:12:27,340 --> 00:12:31,460
They came and reported what they had seen of angels who said he was alive.

272
00:12:32,060 --> 00:12:33,640
Some of those who were with us went to the tomb,

273
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:35,500
and they found it as just as the women said.

274
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:36,860
But they didn't see him.

275
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:38,080
And he said to them,

276
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:38,960
How foolish are you,

277
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:41,880
and how slow to believe all that the prophets have spoken.

278
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:46,500
Wasn't it necessary for the Messiah to suffer these things and enter into his glory?

279
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:48,680
Then, beginning with Moses and the prophets,

280
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:51,860
he interpreted for them the things concerning himself and all the Scriptures.

281
00:12:51,860 --> 00:12:55,860
And I lost where I was at.

282
00:12:55,860 --> 00:12:56,980
Oh, 28.

283
00:12:56,980 --> 00:12:59,320
They came near to the village where they were going,

284
00:12:59,320 --> 00:13:01,320
and he gave the impression that he was going further.

285
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:02,560
But they urged him,

286
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:04,380
Stay with us because it's almost evening,

287
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:05,900
and now the day is almost over.

288
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:07,200
So he went in to stay with them.

289
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:10,600
It was as he reclined at the table with them,

290
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:14,700
he took the bread and blessed it and broke it and gave it to them.

291
00:13:14,700 --> 00:13:17,240
Then their eyes were opened,

292
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:18,440
and they recognized him,

293
00:13:18,440 --> 00:13:20,180
but he disappeared from their sight.

294
00:13:20,180 --> 00:13:21,560
They said to each other,

295
00:13:21,560 --> 00:13:23,340
Weren't our hearts burning within us

296
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:24,620
while he was talking with us on the road

297
00:13:24,620 --> 00:13:26,040
and explaining the Scriptures to us?

298
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:27,180
That very hour,

299
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:29,060
they got up and they returned to Jerusalem.

300
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:32,540
They found the eleven and those with them gathered together who said,

301
00:13:32,540 --> 00:13:36,300
The Lord has truly been raised and has appeared to Simon.

302
00:13:36,300 --> 00:13:39,920
Then they began to describe what had happened on the road

303
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:43,320
and how he was made known to them in the breaking of the bread.

304
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:45,300
As they were saying these things,

305
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:47,920
he himself stood in their midst and he said to them,

306
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:48,780
Peace to you.

307
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:51,220
But they were startled and terrified.

308
00:13:51,220 --> 00:13:52,480
They thought they were seeing a ghost.

309
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:54,120
Why are you troubled?

310
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:54,940
He asked them.

311
00:13:55,980 --> 00:13:57,880
And why do doubts arise in your heart?

312
00:13:57,880 --> 00:14:00,140
Look at my hands and my feet.

313
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:01,720
That is it.

314
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:02,380
I myself.

315
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:04,540
Touch me and see.

316
00:14:04,540 --> 00:14:08,280
Because a ghost does not have flesh and bones as you can see I have.

317
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:09,980
Having said this,

318
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:11,400
he showed them his hands and his feet.

319
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:14,000
But while they were amazed and disbelieved because of their joy,

320
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:14,660
he asked them,

321
00:14:14,660 --> 00:14:16,460
Do you guys have anything to eat?

322
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:20,120
So they gave him a piece of broiled fish and he took them

323
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:22,140
and he took it and ate in their presence.

324
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:23,140
He told them,

325
00:14:23,140 --> 00:14:25,760
These are my words that I spoke to you while I was still with you,

326
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:27,840
that everything written about me in the law of Moses,

327
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:29,920
the prophets, and the Psalms must be fulfilled.

328
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:32,440
Then he opened their minds to understand the Scriptures.

329
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:33,560
And he also said to them,

330
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:34,940
This is what is written.

331
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:38,040
The Messiah will suffer and rise from the dead on the third day.

332
00:14:38,040 --> 00:14:40,340
And repentance for forgiveness of sins

333
00:14:40,340 --> 00:14:43,960
will be proclaimed in his name to all the nations beginning in Jerusalem.

334
00:14:43,960 --> 00:14:47,580
You are witnesses of these things.

335
00:14:47,820 --> 00:14:50,320
And look, I am sending you what my father has promised.

336
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:53,680
As for you, stay in the city until you are empowered from on high.

337
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:56,920
Then he led them out of the vicinity of Bethany.

338
00:14:56,920 --> 00:14:58,440
And lifting up his hands, he blessed them.

339
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:01,280
And while he was blessing them, he left them and was carried up into heaven.

340
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:04,420
After worshiping him, they returned to Jerusalem with great joy.

341
00:15:04,420 --> 00:15:08,040
And they were continually in the temple praising God.

342
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:14,460
Now, I'll fully admit, I think the millennials get a bad rap.

343
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:18,100
Sometimes we deserve it.

344
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:21,700
But we're really good at redefining terms.

345
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:25,040
You other generations have also redefined terms.

346
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:26,140
So let's not get judgy.

347
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:30,060
But let's start here.

348
00:15:30,060 --> 00:15:31,400
Let's start at a definition.

349
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:32,580
Resurrection.

350
00:15:32,580 --> 00:15:34,960
According to the Britannica Encyclopedia,

351
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:39,420
resurrection means the rising from the dead of a divine or human being

352
00:15:39,420 --> 00:15:43,380
who still retains his or her own personhood or individuality,

353
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:45,820
though the body may or may not be changed.

354
00:15:45,820 --> 00:15:49,060
See, my fear is, in the Western church,

355
00:15:49,060 --> 00:15:51,960
we've made the resurrection of Jesus all spiritual, in a sense.

356
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,740
Where we've adopted Greek and Roman philosophy

357
00:15:54,740 --> 00:15:58,640
and subconsciously integrated it into our doctrine of resurrection.

358
00:15:59,580 --> 00:16:03,560
Where the philosophy of Plato and his theory of the soul and its perceived immortality

359
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:05,620
and pushing that theory out to its conclusion

360
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:09,100
have done more to drive home the pictures of our post-mortem existence

361
00:16:09,100 --> 00:16:10,860
than Jesus' own words.

362
00:16:12,220 --> 00:16:15,040
These philosophies lead us to a place where we start to question

363
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:17,080
if Jesus' resurrection was bodily or not.

364
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:20,820
Where we question if our promised resurrection will be bodily or not.

365
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:23,180
See, most Christians today, I fear,

366
00:16:23,180 --> 00:16:26,860
never spend time to think through the implications of the resurrection

367
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:28,660
from one Easter season to the next.

368
00:16:28,660 --> 00:16:33,540
We remain satisfied with what is at best a distorted version of the great biblical hope.

369
00:16:33,540 --> 00:16:36,860
Where we've distorted the idea of heaven and resurrection

370
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:38,660
into things the Bible doesn't articulate.

371
00:16:38,660 --> 00:16:42,120
Where pop culture speaks more into our understanding

372
00:16:42,120 --> 00:16:44,060
of life after death than the Scriptures do.

373
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:47,340
Where resurrection from the biblical world is this.

374
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:52,140
Jesus was crucified, he died, he was buried in a tomb,

375
00:16:52,140 --> 00:16:53,840
and on the third day he arose.

376
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:56,080
Not spiritually, where his body was still there

377
00:16:56,080 --> 00:16:57,500
and his soul was somewhere else,

378
00:16:57,500 --> 00:17:00,200
but both physically and spiritually, his whole person.

379
00:17:00,200 --> 00:17:02,420
Where the burial linens were neatly wrapped

380
00:17:02,420 --> 00:17:04,060
and laid where his body was.

381
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:06,000
So within the biblical world,

382
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,460
resurrection in both the Greek, Latin, or other language equivalents

383
00:17:09,460 --> 00:17:11,540
was never used to mean life after.

384
00:17:12,020 --> 00:17:16,820
Death, resurrection was used to denote a new bodily life

385
00:17:16,820 --> 00:17:19,780
after whatever sort of life after death there might be.

386
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:22,360
See, the ancients spoke of resurrection,

387
00:17:22,360 --> 00:17:24,520
whether it was to deny it like all pagans did,

388
00:17:24,520 --> 00:17:27,500
or to affirm it like some Jews did,

389
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:29,340
they were referring to a two-step narrative

390
00:17:29,340 --> 00:17:32,260
in which resurrection, meaning a new bodily life,

391
00:17:32,260 --> 00:17:35,480
would be preceded by an interim period of bodily death.

392
00:17:35,960 --> 00:17:40,920
When authors like Luke, Matthew, Mark, and John penned the events around the resurrection,

393
00:17:40,920 --> 00:17:45,280
when new converts said that Jesus had been risen from the dead,

394
00:17:45,280 --> 00:17:47,380
this is what I think they were saying.

395
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:52,360
Something had happened to him that has happened to nobody else,

396
00:17:52,360 --> 00:17:54,540
and that nobody expected to happen.

397
00:17:54,540 --> 00:17:58,260
So they weren't talking about Jesus' soul going to ethereal bliss,

398
00:17:58,260 --> 00:18:03,340
nor were they saying confusedly that Jesus had somehow become one with his divine nature.

399
00:18:03,340 --> 00:18:08,640
See, some Jews even agreed with the pagans who denied any kind of future life,

400
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:10,020
especially a re-embodied one.

401
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:14,780
For my note-takers, if you want to check out the definition of the Sadducees in Mark 12, 18.

402
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:18,560
But other groups fought with the pagans

403
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:22,160
in terms of a glorious though disembodied future for the soul.

404
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:27,660
But most of the first century Jews believed in an eventual resurrection,

405
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:30,440
especially the Pharisees, which get a bad rap,

406
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:33,380
sometimes well-deserved, like the millennials.

407
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:38,500
But which in this group, this is what the Apostle Paul would have learned under.

408
00:18:38,940 --> 00:18:40,340
Resurrection in a biblical sense,

409
00:18:40,340 --> 00:18:43,040
where God would be the caretaker of their soul,

410
00:18:43,040 --> 00:18:44,940
or in the Greek, their psyche,

411
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:47,120
after death until the last day,

412
00:18:47,120 --> 00:18:49,160
where God would give his new people new bodies

413
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:51,160
when he judged and remade the whole world.

414
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:56,460
See, this is what I think Martha assumes Jesus is talking about

415
00:18:56,460 --> 00:18:59,280
in their conversation at the tomb of Lazarus in John 14.

416
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:03,300
We start, if you want to turn in your Bibles to John 14,

417
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:04,420
starting in verse 21,

418
00:19:04,420 --> 00:19:06,300
Martha says to Jesus,

419
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,340
If you had been here, my brother wouldn't have died.

420
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:13,400
Yet, even now I know that whatever you ask from God,

421
00:19:13,400 --> 00:19:14,720
God will give you.

422
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:18,100
Your brother will rise again, Jesus said.

423
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:19,860
Notice the words of Martha here.

424
00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:20,800
Martha says to him,

425
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:26,100
I know that he will rise again in the resurrection at the last day.

426
00:19:26,100 --> 00:19:27,680
And Jesus said to her,

427
00:19:27,680 --> 00:19:29,220
I am the resurrection and the life.

428
00:19:29,220 --> 00:19:30,800
I am the one that is pointing to.

429
00:19:30,800 --> 00:19:32,400
The one who believes in me,

430
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:34,040
even if he dies, will live.

431
00:19:34,040 --> 00:19:38,260
See, this is what I think resurrection meant to the first century audience.

432
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:41,160
That there are two parts to life after death.

433
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:44,180
One, which we're really good at remembering as Christians, right?

434
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:47,560
That at death, we are immediately present with the Lord.

435
00:19:47,560 --> 00:19:50,200
But the second part is really the crux of my sermon.

436
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:53,980
That's the eventual future that God will reunite body and soul.

437
00:19:53,980 --> 00:19:55,660
And I don't want you to miss this.

438
00:19:55,660 --> 00:19:58,580
The resurrection means reuniting body and soul.

439
00:19:58,580 --> 00:20:03,580
The whole person perfected, glorified the way God always intended us to be.

440
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:05,200
Biblical resurrection.

441
00:20:06,360 --> 00:20:11,860
So the heart of this message is to help you navigate the value I see in a resurrection worldview.

442
00:20:11,860 --> 00:20:16,180
Where I see shortcomings, and you guys can write me emails, don't write Ryan.

443
00:20:16,180 --> 00:20:19,060
Where I see shortcomings in the we die, we go to heaven argument,

444
00:20:19,060 --> 00:20:22,040
I see fulfillment in the resurrection.

445
00:20:22,040 --> 00:20:25,660
And where unanswered prayers,

446
00:20:25,660 --> 00:20:31,040
where prayers seem unanswered for someone who experiences sickness, pain, or death,

447
00:20:31,040 --> 00:20:32,840
or they just die and go to heaven,

448
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,620
resurrection for me is, this is not the end.

449
00:20:37,620 --> 00:20:42,100
Poverty, violence, racism, pain, sickness, and death

450
00:20:42,100 --> 00:20:44,560
may be a byproduct of a fallen world,

451
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:48,120
but through Jesus' resurrection and our eventual one,

452
00:20:48,120 --> 00:20:51,160
all the wrongs of the world will be set right through Jesus.

453
00:20:51,160 --> 00:20:56,060
We'll be able to witness and experience God's justice and mercy on full display

454
00:20:56,060 --> 00:21:00,200
in a world fully under the reign of King Jesus,

455
00:21:00,200 --> 00:21:04,860
where sickness, pain, death are all done away with.

456
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,260
And we see the justice of God dissolve those broken things away.

457
00:21:09,260 --> 00:21:12,300
I love how N.T. Wright says this,

458
00:21:12,300 --> 00:21:17,400
the resurrection declared that Jesus was not the ordinary sort of political king,

459
00:21:17,400 --> 00:21:19,780
a rebel leader that some had supposed.

460
00:21:19,780 --> 00:21:26,100
He was the leader of a far larger, more radical revolution than anyone had ever supposed.

461
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:32,380
He was inaugurating a new world, a new creation, a new way of being human.

462
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:38,820
He was forging a way into a new cosmos, a new era, a form of existence hinted at all along,

463
00:21:38,820 --> 00:21:41,560
but never before unveiled.

464
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:44,380
Are we tracking together?

465
00:21:44,380 --> 00:21:47,620
Are we challenged just a little bit in our thinking?

466
00:21:47,620 --> 00:21:50,560
I get nothing.

467
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:52,220
So I'm going to assume that's good.

468
00:21:52,220 --> 00:21:54,840
Let's turn now to 1 Corinthians 15.

469
00:21:54,840 --> 00:21:57,580
It's one of my favorite chapters in all the Bible.

470
00:21:57,580 --> 00:21:59,860
And I agree with Ryan what he said last week.

471
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:01,060
The Corinthian church is a mess.

472
00:22:01,060 --> 00:22:01,660
So are we.

473
00:22:01,860 --> 00:22:03,840
So why not have hope in the resurrection?

474
00:22:03,840 --> 00:22:07,860
1 Corinthians 15.

475
00:22:07,860 --> 00:22:08,960
We'll start at verse 1.

476
00:22:08,960 --> 00:22:12,440
Now I want to make clear in your little header there.

477
00:22:12,440 --> 00:22:18,120
Resurrection essential to the gospel or something to that effect, depending on your translation.

478
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:22,020
Now I want to make clear for you, brothers and sisters, the gospel I preached to you,

479
00:22:22,020 --> 00:22:26,780
which you received and have taken your stand and by which you are being saved.

480
00:22:26,780 --> 00:22:30,240
You hold to the message I preached to you unless you believed in vain.

481
00:22:30,780 --> 00:22:33,760
For I passed on to you as the most important of what I received.

482
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:35,620
Remember Ryan's words on unity.

483
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:36,980
These are non-negotiables.

484
00:22:36,980 --> 00:22:40,180
That Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures.

485
00:22:40,180 --> 00:22:44,360
That he was buried and that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures.

486
00:22:44,360 --> 00:22:46,000
That he appeared to Cephas.

487
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:46,540
That's Peter.

488
00:22:46,540 --> 00:22:47,940
Then to the 12.

489
00:22:47,940 --> 00:22:51,880
Then he appeared to over 500 brothers and sisters at one time.

490
00:22:51,880 --> 00:22:54,340
Most of them are still alive, but some have fallen asleep.

491
00:22:54,340 --> 00:22:56,480
What Paul's saying is, go check it out.

492
00:22:56,480 --> 00:22:58,220
Like, test this claim.

493
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:02,080
Then he appeared to James, to the apostles.

494
00:23:02,080 --> 00:23:07,060
And last of all, as one being born at the wrong time, he also appeared to me.

495
00:23:07,060 --> 00:23:11,620
Remember, Paul's kind of referencing back to his road to Damascus experience.

496
00:23:11,620 --> 00:23:16,600
And I would even say in Galatians 1, his experience in the Arabian desert being taught by Jesus.

497
00:23:17,880 --> 00:23:24,300
Verse 9, for I am the least of the apostles, not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.

498
00:23:24,300 --> 00:23:29,400
But by the grace of God, I am what I am, and his grace towards me was not in vain.

499
00:23:29,400 --> 00:23:32,960
On the contrary, I worked harder than any of them.

500
00:23:32,960 --> 00:23:34,720
That's a tough claim.

501
00:23:35,260 --> 00:23:37,960
Yet not I, but the grace of God that was in me.

502
00:23:37,960 --> 00:23:44,180
Whether then it is I or they, so we proclaim, and so you have believed.

503
00:23:44,180 --> 00:23:49,520
The header there, resurrection essential to the faith.

504
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:53,480
See, remember the beginning of the sermon, faith being our interpretation of reality.

505
00:23:53,480 --> 00:24:01,060
